
## 1. What does “in-place” mean?

An in-place operation modifies the existing list instead of creating another list to hold the result.

Suppose you have:

```
numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40]
```

You want to change the first element from `10` to `99`.

```
numbers[0] = 99print(numbers)
```

Output:

```
[99, 20, 30, 40]
```

You modified the existing list directly. You did not need to create a second list.

## 2. In-place vs creating a new list

### In-place modification

```
numbers = [1, 2, 3]numbers[0] = 99
```

One list is modified.

### Creating a new list

```
numbers = [1, 2, 3]new_numbers = [99, 2, 3]
```

Two separate lists exist.

Why does this matter in interviews? Some problems explicitly require you to modify the original list and use constant extra space, \\(O(1)\\).

## 3. Common in-place operations

### A. Updating an element

```
numbers = [5, 10, 15]numbers[1] = 100
```

Result: `[5, 100, 15]`

### B. Swapping two elements

```
numbers = [5, 10, 15]numbers[0], numbers[2] = numbers[2], numbers[0]
```

Result: `[15, 10, 5]`

This is the same swapping technique you used when reversing arrays.

### C. Reversing a list

```
numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]left = 0right = len(numbers) - 1while left < right:    numbers[left], numbers[right] = numbers[right], numbers[left]    left += 1    right -= 1
```

The original list is reversed without creating another list.

### D. Removing an element

Python provides `pop()` to remove an element from a list.

```
numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40]numbers.pop(1)print(numbers)
```

Output: `[10, 30, 40]`

The element at index `1`, which is `20`, is removed. The remaining elements shift to fill the gap.

Note: `pop()` modifies the original list, but removing an element from the middle generally takes \\(O(n)\\) time because other elements must shift.

## 4. Time and space complexity

| Operation                         | Time complexity | Extra space                |
| --------------------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------- |
| Update an element by index        | \\(O(1)\\)      | \\(O(1)\\)                 |
| Swap two elements                 | \\(O(1)\\)      | \\(O(1)\\)                 |
| Reverse the list using swaps      | \\(O(n)\\)      | \\(O(1)\\)                 |
| Remove an element from the middle | \\(O(n)\\)      | \\(O(1)\\) auxiliary space |

Important distinction: In-place does not mean the operation takes \\(O(1)\\) time. Reversing a list is in-place, but still takes \\(O(n)\\) time.

Also, auxiliary space means extra working memory. A Python list itself still occupies \\(O(n)\\) space; in-place algorithms simply avoid allocating another list of size \\(n\\).

